Marc Andreessen was born in 1971 and grew up in New Lisbon, Wisconsin. He discovered programming at age 12. While studying computer science at the University of Illinois, he worked as a programmer at the National Center for Supercomputing Applications (NCSA).

## Mosaic

Early browsers were hard to use and showed only text. In late 1992 Andreessen and his NCSA colleague Eric Bina set out to build a browser that was easy to install, ran on many kinds of computers, and showed images right alongside the text. They released Mosaic in 1993. It was the first browser to be widely used on both Windows and Mac, and it helped the web grow from a few dozen sites to thousands within about a year.

## Netscape

- **1994:** Co-founded Netscape with Jim Clark. Its Navigator browser became the first big commercial browser.
- **1994:** Named one of six inductees to the World Wide Web Hall of Fame at the first international web conference.
- **1995:** Netscape's stock market debut kicked off the dot-com boom. That same year, Netscape hired Brendan Eich, who created JavaScript for Navigator.
- **2009:** Co-founded the venture capital firm Andreessen Horowitz with Ben Horowitz.
- **2013:** Shared the first Queen Elizabeth Prize for Engineering with other internet and web pioneers.

Netscape's source code later became the starting point for the Mozilla project and the Firefox browser.

